OODA

File: openOODA/OODA.oot
One rotation: Observe, Decide, Act, Lock, Ship.

Always load
  RULES.oot — how to work
  SWARM.oot — how to use helper agents
  LORE.oot — hard-won lessons
  SHIPPED.oot — what exists

Also
  DESIGN.oot — what we want
  PM.oot — pin
  SPRINT.oot — this week
  PROBE.oot — when a claim needs a hostile check
  SCRIPTING.oot — when the work is a smoke or a .sh replacement (same .oo, not .oos)

SWARM every run
  Light work uses light agents. Heavy design uses heavy agents.
  Give each helper one tight job. Stop at the goal or the first hard fail.
  If stuck, probe with simple tools. More agents on one stuck bug wastes tokens.
  Two helpers must not edit the same files unless they use two separate copies.
  A helper may attack claims. If a hole lets bad work pass: close it, stop, report.
  Solo is allowed when SWARM would waste effort (one tiny file, one command).
  Even then: read SWARM and say "solo under SWARM" plus why.

Adapter, fill every session
  ROOT:
  Product binaries / how to build and run:
  Backlog file:
  Handoff file:
  Rails this ROOT actually has:
  Hygiene commands:
  DESIGN present:
  SWARM plan: solo or fan-out (roles and files each owns)

1. Observe
  Read the handoff (SPRINT / notes).
  Read the pin (PM).
  Skim LORE so you do not repeat a past mistake.
  Check file size, junk files, and failed tests.
  If this product has I/O, exec, or secrets, look at those surfaces.
  Skim SWARM: is this turn multi-file? Note fan-out or solo and why.
  Ask: what is the best next product step?
  Plan only enough to Act.

2. Decide (at most 5)
  Prefer, in order:
    1. Failed tests and broken product checks
    2. Honesty / security holes that let bad work pass
    3. Next backlog item that serves DESIGN
    4. DESIGN gap — add to backlog, then do
    5. Hygiene only if it blocks or is cheap
  Also decide SWARM shape:
    Solo — one agent; reason fits SWARM
    Fan-out — few helpers; each owns different files or a read-only role
    Never many agents on one stuck bug

3. Act
  Implement on the real product path.
  Run under the SWARM plan from Decide.
  If work is not done, leave it safe or name what is left.
  Keep files under 256 lines or split now.
  Ship tests with behavior when claims matter.
  No secrets in the tree.
  Owner notes stay plain English.

4. Lock
  Run RULES 1.7: line size, cruft, secrets, temps, docs match code.
  Run only the rails this ROOT actually has.
  Check a box only if it is really done.
  No leftover rogue helpers. No two agents left on the same files.
  Verify claims yourself.
  If hygiene fails, do not Ship. Fix it or name the leftover.

5. Ship
  Commit as appropriate. No secrets. No force-push of shared main unless the owner said so.
  Short report: what changed, why it matters, what is still not done.
  Note SWARM shape: solo (reason) or fan-out: roles.

When to run PROBE
  A security or privilege claim needs a hostile check.
  You suspect a hole, torn state, or a fake green rail.
  After a large change to I/O, auth, exec, or caps.
  PROBE adds hostility. It does not replace Lock.
  PROBE workers still follow SWARM.
